# Seven animals from the picture cards, and I have for having a pet.
lesson language-animals v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=4 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[animal.i-have-a-dog|cap]]"
  goal "Name seven animals in [[language.name]], and say which pet you have."

  look {
    scene 37x9 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        picturecards pets who=["cat", "dog", "duck", "rabbit"] where=["by", "by", "by", "by"] thing=["box", "box", "box", "box"] letters=false
      }
    }
    say """
      At the ferry town [[animal.the-cat]] sleeps on a warm step, [[animal.the-dog]] runs by the
      harbour, [[animal.the-duck]] swims and [[animal.the-rabbit]] hides in the garden. To say you
      have a pet, say [[phrase.i-have-a-dog]], I have a dog.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.animals-name k=3
    }
    show language.animals-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.animals-meaning k=4 easy-k=2
    show language.animals-write k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.animals-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.animals-match k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show language.animals-write k=5 easy-k=3 hard-k=4
    show language.animals-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.animals-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.animals-write k=2
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-animals-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.animals-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[animal.the-cat|cap]], [[animal.the-dog]], [[animal.the-duck]], [[animal.the-rabbit]], [[animal.the-pig]], [[animal.the-fox]], [[animal.the-hen]]."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is two animal words that look alike taken for each other, and
    leaving out the little word that goes before a noun. The picture cards draw each animal the same
    way every time, so the picture carries the answer to the naming questions and the child's job is
    the word. The written word is asked without the little word before it. The listening question is
    a dictation: read the sentence aloud from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, slowly and
    twice, or let the device play it. It is marked word by word, and a missing accent counts. If the
    child is stuck, make the animal's noise, then say its name together. Off the page, play animal
    snap with the names in the language. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3
    to 6, statutory from age 7): "broaden their vocabulary"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language
    activities: "Activities to listen to simple words and phrases and basic expressions about their
    familiar people and their surroundings and match the phrases and expressions with illustrations
    and photographs"; and CEFR A1, "Can understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very
    basic phrases". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[animal.the-rabbit]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[animal.the-cat]] and [[animal.the-dog]] and the words to write are [[animal.duck]] and [[animal.pig]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[animal.the-pig]] and [[animal.the-hen]] and the words to write are [[animal.fox]], [[animal.rabbit]] and [[animal.dog]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
